LIMITATION APPENDED 2026-08-18 (post-batch, by the operator; nothing re-run,
nothing re-costed, no collector filter / guard ceiling / quiet-window logic
changed by this note)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Subject: the post-run baseline contamination recorded against pilot C5 rep2.

  The backfill's post-run baseline probe for that cell saw provider tokens after
  the run's own window closed, and the contamination guard therefore refused the
  cell. That observation is consistent with the run's OWN token ingestion lagging
  into the baseline window: Cloud Monitoring ingestion is not instantaneous, and
  the labels the meter exposes cannot distinguish a late-arriving point belonging
  to our run from a genuinely foreign one (the collector README's
  "model_user_id collapses effort levels" — the same label poverty applies here).
  "Consistent with" is the whole claim. We cannot demonstrate it, because the
  labels that would demonstrate it do not exist.

  Guard behaviour is UNCHANGED and no exception was made:
    * the refusal stands for pilot C5 rep2;
    * its Product-B usage stays "unavailable" with its confidence tier — it is
      NOT zero-filled and NOT imputed from a sibling rep (CLAUDE.md rule 3);
    * the cell is a HOLE in the registered matrix and is reported as one.

  A guard that we relax on the strength of a plausible story is not a guard. If
  the window placement is in fact mis-specified — e.g. the baseline probe should
  start after an ingestion-lag offset rather than at run end — then the fix is to
  change the window and RE-MEASURE, not to accept the number this run produced.
  That review is post-batch work, out of scope for this batch and for the
  relaunch: it is registered here so the decision is on the record rather than
  rediscovered later.

  Scope: this note concerns ONE cell (pilot-realworld C5 rep2) and does not
  reinterpret any other refusal, deferral, or "unavailable" in this dataset.

