--- D (cont.). THE FIRST v2 PASS WAS WRONG TWICE; THIS IS THE CORRECTED ONE -----
2026-08-20T09:56:00Z. Still zero model spend — Cloud Monitoring reads only.

The v2 pass recorded above (flat 300s tail, 2 recovered / 37 refused / 38 skipped)
is SUPERSEDED by the pass recorded here. Two defects, both found by looking at the
meter rather than at the code:

 (i) THE TAIL WAS ASSUMED, NOT MEASURED. Dumping every raw point around
     pilot-realworld-draft-articles__C5__rep2 (run 04:11:33 -> 04:15:38) showed a
     contiguous chain of that run's own points from -100s to +482s past its end
     (gaps <= 120s), then 20 minutes of complete silence until the next run's
     traffic. The run's container is destroyed at run end, so contiguous points
     after it can only be its own late-metered calls — and a flat 300s tail is
     SHORTER than this metric's ingestion delay. v2's first pass therefore
     reproduced v1's defect in a milder form.
     Fix: the tail is now measured. It extends while points keep arriving closer
     together than --tail-silence-seconds (300s) and ends at the first silence,
     under a hard --tail-seconds cap (900s). A tail still producing points AT the
     cap is REFUSED, not credited: an ingestion tail decays, a third party working
     does not. The post-run quiet probe widens to the whole no-man's-land after a
     silenced tail, so a third-party burst anywhere before the next run still
     refuses. Recorded per run as tail_ended_by = no_points | silence |
     next_subject_run | tail_cap.

(ii) INSEPARABILITY WAS PROPAGATED TRANSITIVELY. When run i's tail landed inside
     run i+1's guard band, i+1 was flagged — correctly — and then the flag was
     read back while it was being written, so i+2, i+3 ... to the end of the batch
     inherited it. One 4s gap poisoned 14 runs. But run i's flag is a claim about
     the i/i+1 boundary only; whether i+1's own tail is separable from i+2 is a
     different boundary and a different question. Propagation is now exactly one
     hop, from a snapshot of the runs' own flags.

Also fixed, both found by running this pass:
  * urllib surfaces a socket read timeout as a bare TimeoutError, which is not a
    URLError, so it escaped the per-run handler and killed the whole 77-run pass
    mid-flight. Transient failures (read timeout, 429, 5xx) are now retried up to
    4 times with backoff; a 400 or a 401 still fails immediately, because
    retrying a wrong answer only buries the message.
  * a run that a later pass successfully attributes now has its refusal marker
    FROM THAT SAME RULE removed — a marker saying "nothing was written under this
    rule" must not sit beside an event written under it. A v1 marker beside a v2
    event is left alone: that is not a contradiction, the two rules drew different
    windows and the v1 refusal is still true of v1.

