--- F. CEILING v3: A RATE CEILING REPLACES THE FIXED PER-RUN CONSTANT -----------
Zero model spend. Cloud Monitoring reads only, over batch 1's historical windows.

WHY. Section D above left 31 of 35 refusals on the 3,000,000 input-side per-run
plausibility ceiling, all of them long Gemini-executor runs whose totals scale
with duration. A fixed per-run constant cannot distinguish "long run" from
"contaminated window", so it was refusing duration. The quantity that does
distinguish them is the rate.

WHAT WAS MEASURED FIRST (all from batch 1's own artifacts, nothing assumed):
   clean attributed windows (the 4 v2 legs)   347 - 2,199 input-side tokens/s
   the 31 ceiling-refused windows           5,306 - 12,258 input-side tokens/s
   the smoke-test contamination that
     motivated the original guard                    16,693 input-side tokens/s
The refused windows sit a factor of 2+ below the contamination and a factor of
2+ above the clean runs, with no overlap in between.

THE RULE. v3 = serialized_run_ownership_with_rate_ceiling. Same window, same
ownership boundaries and same third-party baseline probe as v2; the ONLY change
is that --ceiling-input-tokens (3M per run) is replaced by
--ceiling-input-tokens-per-second, default 25,000/s, over the attributed window.
It replaces rather than adds: applying both would keep the refusals v3 exists to
stop making.

KNOWN LIMIT, recorded here and in harness/collectors/README.md. 25,000/s is
headroom over observed rates, not a published bound, and it does NOT dominate the
fixed ceiling: the 16,693/s smoke contamination passes it. What refuses that
window under v3 is the unchanged baseline probe (1,274,568 input tokens in the 5
minutes after, 176,672 in the 5 before). The probe, not the ceiling, is v3's
defence against a stranger working at a believable rate, and a regression test
pins that. Revisit 25,000/s if a subject arm is ever observed above ~10,000/s.

